  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 314 PM EDT MON OCT 26 2026\n\nAt the onset of the extended period on Wednesday, an anomalously\nhigh-amplitude upper-level ridge will remain anchored across the\nSoutheast, while broad longwave troughing dominates the western\nCONUS. Across eastern Kentucky, 500 mb heights near 585 dam sit\nwithin the 90th to 95th climatological percentile, highlighting the\nunseasonable nature of this pattern. At the surface, light\nsoutheasterly winds will gradually veer to the south and southwest as\nhigh pressure shifts offshore. A passing weak shortwave aloft will\nbring an increase in mid-and high-level cloudiness on Wednesday, \nfollowed by clearing late in the day as the wave exits. Afternoon \ntemperatures should warm into the lower 70s. With calm winds, dry \nair, and clear skies, strong nocturnal radiational cooling will \nsupport pronounced ridge-valley temperature splits Wednesday \nnight. Sheltered valleys should dip into the low-to-mid 40s, while\nthermal belt ridges hold in the upper 40s, near 50F. Some patchy \nriver valley fog should also develop.\n\nThursday through Friday, the upper ridge axis broadens and expands\nnorthward into the Ohio Valley, allowing deep-layer subsidence to\npeak and maintaining mostly clear skies. Surface high pressure will\nshift directly overhead across the central Appalachians,\nstrengthening to around 1024 mb by Friday morning. Daytime warming\nunder ample sunshine should push afternoon highs into the low to\nmid-70s each day - roughly 5 to 10 degrees above late-October\nnormals.\n\nSaturday through Monday, the upper ridge flattens into more\nprogressive southwest flow aloft. Meanwhile, strong surface high\npressure holds along the East Coast through Sunday, keeping the\napproaching cold front stalled well to our west across the Plains and\nUpper Midwest. This keeps eastern Kentucky firmly in the warm sector,\nwhere persistent southerly flow will pump unseasonable moisture into\nthe region. Precipitable water values are expected to surge into the\n90th climatological percentile from November 1st onward, peaking\naround 1.2 to 1.3 inches on Sunday.\n\nSubtle shortwaves rippling through the flow aloft interacting with\nthis rich moisture will lead to rain chances starting Saturday\nafternoon, with precipitation chances peaking on Sunday around 60%.\nThe front finally edges closer heading into Monday before petering\nout, allowing PoPs to taper down to around 20% by Monday afternoon.\nHighs will hold in the low-to-mid 70s Saturday, then trim back a bit\nfor Sunday and Monday under widespread clouds and scattered rain.",
